the head of uber is responding to a  scathing report about the ride-hailing  companies passenger safety protocols The  Washington Post reported Wednesday that  agents in ubers Special Investigations  Unit are coached to put the company's  reputation ahead of passenger safety  Kris Van Cleave spoke with uber CEO and  joins us now Chris what did he have to  say  well good morning he believes that that  article is based on hearsay uber says  that it used old facts and that the  company's policies and procedures have  evolved particularly in the last few  years now we spoke with Derek his raashi  shortly after uber formally unveiled a  number of new safety measures we first  reported here on CBS this morning an  optional verification system will  require drivers to get a pin code from  their riders before a trip can start  among the other features uber introduced  an in-app function allowing riders to  text 911 emergency the company hopes the  new features will help prevent the type  of mistake police believe Samantha  Josephson made when she appeared to  mistake a car for her uber in March she  was later found murdered now about the  post article uber CEO says if a victim  of sexual assault wants to report the  attack to police uber makes it quote  very easy to do so so we have done  everything that we can we've been  advised by law enforcement we have been  advised by victim advocacy group  advocacy groups to make sure that when  something does happen we respond in a  way that's victim centric that's victim  first what do you say to to these  reports and to folks who have have  either sued you or have suffered a  sexual assault that feel like the  response wasn't there I say that that  report fundamentally was based on  hearsay we're not perfect  I wouldn't claim to be perfect but I  believe that we're significantly better  than we were last year and next year  darn it we're going to be better  uber executives insist if they get a  report about an alleged sexual assault  against a driver that driver is  immediately removed from the system  there is no three strikes policy at uber  they say now Senator Richard Blumenthal  has sent letters to rideshare executives  Express  concern saying the industry needs to do  more Gail  all right Chris thank you very much
 

